/**
 * Full, beginner-friendly in-app manual for all BiForge modes.
 * The same static guide is shown in the modal and in the optional new tab,
 * so the two reading experiences cannot silently drift apart.
 */
import React, { useEffect, useCallback } from 'react';
import { CircleHelp, ExternalLink, X } from 'lucide-react';

const GUIDE_HTML = String.raw`
<h1>Welcome to BiForge</h1>
<p class="lead">This guide explains how to use <strong>all three BiForge agents</strong>: <strong>Conversational</strong>, <strong>Expert</strong>, and <strong>Architect</strong>. You do not need to know anything about artificial intelligence to begin.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>The most important rule:</strong> BiForge can help you think, analyse information, and prepare work. It can make mistakes. <strong>You remain in control.</strong> Read important suggestions, correct anything that is wrong, and make the final decisions yourself.</div>

<nav class="toc" aria-label="Guide contents"><strong>In this guide</strong><ol><li><a href="#start">Start here</a></li><li><a href="#basics">How to work with an AI assistant</a></li><li><a href="#modes">Choose the right mode</a></li><li><a href="#simple">Conversational: step by step</a></li><li><a href="#expert">Expert agent: the decision method</a></li><li><a href="#project">Architect: build and manage a project</a></li><li><a href="#files">Files and attachments</a></li><li><a href="#topbar">Top bar, history, import and export</a></li><li><a href="#privacy">Privacy and safe use</a></li><li><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li><li><a href="#glossary">Small glossary</a></li><li><a href="#lab-escape">Your first project: build Lab Escape</a></li></ol></nav>

<h2 id="start">1. Start here</h2>
<ol>
<li>Open BiForge in your web browser.</li>
<li>On the start screen, choose the mode that best matches what you want to do. If you are unsure, begin with <strong>Conversational</strong>.</li>
<li>Type your request in ordinary language. You do not need special commands or technical vocabulary.</li>
<li>Press <strong>Send</strong> (or the equivalent action shown on the screen) and wait for the response.</li>
<li>Read the response. If it is unclear, incomplete, or based on a wrong assumption, say so and explain what needs to change.</li>
</ol>
<p>You can ask follow-up questions. You do not have to get the wording perfect on your first try. For example, you can say: <em>“Please explain that in simpler English,” “Give me an example,” “You misunderstood the second point,”</em> or <em>“Ask me one question at a time.”</em></p>

<h2 id="basics">2. How to work with an AI assistant</h2>
<h3>Give enough context</h3><p>A useful request usually says what you want, why you want it, and any important limits. Compare these examples:</p>
<div class="example"><p><strong>Too vague:</strong> “Help me with a website.”</p><p><strong>More useful:</strong><span> “I want a small website for a local gardening club. Visitors should be able to read announcements and send a</span><span> contact message. I have no coding experience. Please explain the simplest approach and its likely costs.”</span></p></div>
<h3>Work in small steps</h3><p><span>For a large task, ask BiForge to start with an outline, then improve one part at a time. You can</span><span> ask it to stop after each step so you can review the result.</span></p>
<h3>Correct mistakes directly</h3><p><span>If a response is wrong, do not assume the assistant will notice by itself. Point to the exact sentence or</span><span> claim and provide the correction or the missing information.</span></p>
<h3>Ask for uncertainty to be explained</h3><p>Ask which statements are known, which are assumptions, and what needs checking. A confident-sounding answer is not automatically a correct answer.</p>
<h3>Know what “Send” does</h3><p><span>Sending a message asks the assistant to process it. It does not mean you have approved the answer, accepted a</span><span> project change, published a website, or authorised a real-world action.</span></p>

<h2 id="modes">3. Choose the right mode</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Use it when…</th><th>How it feels</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Conversational</strong></td><td>You have a question, need an explanation, want to draft text, or need practical help.</td><td>Like a normal conversation. No required sequence of stages.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Expert</strong></td><td>You face an important decision and want to examine options, challenge assumptions, test ideas, and record what you learn.</td><td>A guided, step-by-step decision process. You move forward when you are ready.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Architect</strong></td><td>You want to define a project, organise requirements and risks, create a plan, track approvals and tasks, or prepare a source-code package.</td><td>A separate workspace with project records and explicit human confirmations.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Use the <strong>Mode</strong> control in the top bar to switch between available modes. Choose <strong>Back to mode selection</strong><span> to return to the start screen. If Architect is shown as unavailable, this BiForge installation has not enabled it; this</span><span> is not a problem with your browser.</span></p>

<h2 id="simple">4. Conversational: step by step</h2>
<h3>4.1 Start a conversation</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Conversational</strong>.</li><li>If you see the empty start screen, select <strong>Start conversation</strong>.</li><li>Type one clear request into the message box.</li><li>Press <strong>Send</strong>.</li><li>Read the answer and continue the conversation as needed.</li></ol>
<h3>4.2 Improve the answer</h3><p>Use follow-up messages to narrow or correct the response. Examples:</p><ul><li>“Keep the answer under 200 words.”</li><li>“Assume I am a complete beginner.”</li><li>“Separate confirmed facts from assumptions.”</li><li>“Give me three alternatives and explain the trade-offs without choosing for me.”</li><li>“Please revise the previous answer using this correction: …”</li></ul>
<h3>4.3 Start a fresh conversation</h3><p>Choose <strong>New</strong> in the top bar. This starts a new dialogue rather than continuing the current thread. If you need the earlier conversation later, check <strong>History</strong> or export a copy first.</p>
<h3>4.4 Use the Conversational agent for everyday work</h3><p><span>The Conversational agent is a good place to ask for explanations, brainstorm ideas, translate or improve text, prepare a checklist, compare</span><span> options, or get help understanding a document. For high-impact choices, ask for risks and uncertainties and verify important facts independently.</span></p>

<h2 id="expert">5. Expert agent: the decision method</h2>
<p>The Expert agent is designed for decisions that deserve more care than a quick chat. It guides you through five broad loops: <strong>Understand</strong> (make the situation clear), <strong>Expand</strong> (explore options), <strong>Attack</strong> (stress-test assumptions), <strong>Verify</strong> (test ideas and evidence), and <strong>Learn</strong> (record the decision, plan, and lessons). The exact screen may use labels such as <strong>Your story</strong>, <strong>Clarify</strong>, <strong>Options</strong>, <strong>Stress-test</strong>, <strong>Test ideas</strong>, <strong>Decide</strong>, <strong>Plan</strong>, and <strong>Learn</strong>.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Important:</strong><span> The Expert agent does not make the decision for you. The assistant helps structure the thinking; you supply your values and</span><span> constraints, review the evidence, and own the final choice.</span></div>
<h3>5.1 Describe the decision</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Expert</strong> from the Mode menu.</li><li>Describe the situation in your own words. Say what decision you face and what makes it difficult.</li><li>Include a deadline, budget, people affected, or non-negotiable constraints if they matter.</li><li>Read the resulting summary. Correct it if it changes your meaning or leaves out something important.</li></ol>
<h3>5.2 Understand and clarify</h3><p><span>The assistant helps separate facts, unknowns, assumptions, goals, and constraints. Answer questions when you can. If you do not know</span><span> an answer, say “I don’t know” rather than guessing. Check that the assistant’s description is fair and neutral before relying</span><span> on it.</span></p>
<h3>5.3 Explore options</h3><p><span>Review the possible paths. Add options the assistant missed, remove unrealistic options, and explain practical limits. You do not have</span><span> to accept every suggestion just because it appears on screen.</span></p>
<h3>5.4 Stress-test the options</h3><p><span>Look for objections, failure scenarios, hidden costs, and assumptions that could be wrong. Consider what might make each option fail.</span><span> The purpose is not to be negative; it is to discover problems while there is still time to respond.</span></p>
<h3>5.5 Test ideas and evidence</h3><p><span>Where appropriate, define what evidence would support or weaken an idea. Ask what can be checked, what result would count</span><span> as success, and what would change your mind. Do not treat an untested prediction as a fact.</span></p>
<h3>5.6 Decide</h3><p><span>Review the trade-offs and record your own decision, including the reason if useful. The assistant may help make the wording</span><span> clearer, but it must not substitute its preferences for yours.</span></p>
<h3>5.7 Create and review the plan</h3><p><span>Read the proposed work plan. Edit it until the actions, owners, timing, and conditions are realistic. Export it if you</span><span> need to share or store it elsewhere.</span></p>
<h3>5.8 Learn from the outcome</h3><p><span>When you have new evidence or the result of your decision, record what happened and what differed from expectations. You</span><span> can start another cycle if the situation has changed or a new decision is needed.</span></p>
<h3>5.9 Move through the stages</h3><ul><li>Use the Expert stage navigation to revisit earlier stages when available.</li><li><span>The method does not automatically move forward just because you chat. Complete or review the current stage and use the</span><span> stage controls when you are ready.</span></li><li>The open conversation area remains available for questions and clarifications during the process.</li><li>If you are unsure what a stage asks for, ask BiForge to explain the task in plain English before proceeding.</li></ul>
<h3>5.10 Save or remove a decision dialogue</h3><p>Use the top-bar export options to save a copy. The Expert agent also provides a <strong>Decision brief</strong> in <strong>More</strong> when enough information exists. Use <strong>Delete dialogue</strong> only when you really want to remove that dialogue from the local workspace.</p>

<h2 id="project">6. Architect: build and manage a project</h2>
<p><span>Architect is a separate workspace. It may require you to sign in with the application password configured by the</span><span> person who runs this BiForge installation. If you do not have access, ask that administrator. Architect can be disabled</span><span> on some installations.</span></p>
<h3>6.1 Sign in</h3><ol><li>Open <strong>Architect</strong> from the Mode menu.</li><li>If prompted, enter the application password supplied by the administrator and choose <strong>Sign in</strong>.</li><li>Never send that password to the AI in a chat message.</li></ol>
<h3>6.2 Start a project (one flow)</h3><p>Architect uses one flow only. There is no beginner or professional mode switch.</p><p>When no project is selected, guided start asks for your idea and a few practical choices.</p><p>You review a short summary, then may create a source ZIP if the server allows it.</p><p>After a project exists, the same workspace offers analysis, plans, tasks, and delivery tools.</p>
<h3>6.3 Guided start</h3><ol><li><strong>What do you want to build?</strong><span> Describe the idea in everyday language. Include the main purpose and what a user should be able to do. You</span><span> need at least a short description before Next becomes available.</span></li><li><strong>Choose the product type.</strong> Select the closest match from the choices shown. If none is perfect, choose the nearest one and clarify the difference in your idea.</li><li><strong>Answer the practical setup questions.</strong><span> Choose whether people need to sign in, whether the app needs to store data in a database, and the interface</span><span> language (English, Russian, or another/mixed language). If you are unsure, choose “Not sure” rather than guessing. Add optional constraints such</span><span> as “must be free to run,” “must work on a phone,” or “no paid APIs.”</span></li><li><strong>Choose a technical shape.</strong> If you do not understand the options, leave <strong>Choose for me</strong> selected. You can change this later with more knowledge.</li><li><strong>Review the summary.</strong> Check that the generated goal, inputs and outputs, and constraints describe what you actually want.</li><li>Select <strong>Create project &amp; build ZIP</strong>. Wait for the result. Do not close the page while it is working.</li><li>If a source ZIP is produced, choose <strong>Download source ZIP</strong> and save it somewhere you can find again. Read the deployment instructions displayed below the download link.</li><li>Use the project list and workspace for further analysis or professional tools when needed.</li></ol>
<p>A ZIP file is a package of files, not a live website. Creating or downloading it does <strong>not</strong> publish it to the internet, upload it to GitHub, or deploy it to a hosting provider.</p>
<h3>6.4 Professional path: create a project</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Professional</strong>.</li><li>Enter a short, recognisable project name. This field is required.</li><li>Optionally enter the main goal. Be specific about the outcome you want.</li><li>Choose <strong>Create project</strong>.</li><li>Select the project in the project list to open its details. Choose <strong>All projects</strong> to return to the list.</li></ol>
<h3>6.5 Review the project description</h3><p>The project description is the shared starting point for later analysis. Review the <strong>Goal</strong> field. Open <strong>Advanced parameters</strong><span> to review the project type, intended users, inputs and outputs, constraints, security and data requirements, success criteria, request/token/code-run limits, how</span><span> code is written, and how involved you want to be.</span></p><ol><li>Edit any field that is incomplete or incorrect.</li><li>Choose <strong>Save description</strong> to save edits without confirming the description.</li><li>When it accurately represents the project, choose <strong>Confirm description</strong>. Analysis is unavailable until the description is confirmed.</li><li>If you change important requirements later, review and save the description again before relying on subsequent results.</li></ol>
<h3>6.6 Run an analysis</h3><ol><li>In <strong>What should we analyse?</strong>, write one clear question or task. For example: “Identify the main risks and missing requirements before we build the first version.”</li><li>If useful, attach project files in the Workspace files area and confirm that the assistant may read them (see the Files section below).</li><li>Choose <strong>Run analysis</strong> and wait for completion.</li><li>Read the proposed results. The assistant may suggest facts, unknowns, hypotheses, risks, requirements, and other project items.</li><li>Check each suggestion. Select <strong>Confirm</strong> only if it is accurate enough to keep; select <strong>Reject</strong> if it is wrong or unsuitable. A proposed item is not accepted automatically.</li><li>Record a decision made by you or your team in <strong>Your decision</strong>, then choose <strong>Add decision</strong>. This records a human decision; it is not a decision made by the model.</li></ol>
<h3>6.7 Understand the project tabs</h3><ul><li><strong>Results</strong> — review proposed facts, unknowns, hypotheses, risks, requirements, and recorded decisions. Confirm or reject proposed items here.</li><li><strong>Plan</strong> — choose <strong>Create a simple plan</strong> to prepare a proposed plan. Review the plan and any pending approvals. Use <strong>Approve</strong> only when you understand and accept the requested action; use <strong>Reject</strong> when you do not. A plan must be approved before its tasks can run.</li><li><strong>Tasks</strong> (shown within the Plan area) — when a task is ready, choose <strong>Start task</strong> to queue it. After it runs, inspect the actual output. Use <strong>Mark as passed</strong> only after checking the acceptance evidence and criteria. Use <strong>Mark as failed</strong><span> if the criteria were not met. BiForge asks you to enter concrete evidence; do not mark a task as passed</span><span> merely to clear the status.</span></li><li><strong>Code package</strong><span> — review code-generation or delivery information when available. Download the resulting package if offered, inspect it, and follow the manual</span><span> instructions. Capabilities depend on the server configuration.</span></li><li><strong>Files</strong> — open saved project artifacts and versions. If Google Drive is configured, you can explicitly save an artifact to Drive.</li><li><strong>Advanced</strong><span> — exposes raw project JSON. This is for people who understand the data format. Incorrect JSON can damage project state;</span><span> beginners should leave it alone.</span></li></ul>
<h3>6.8 Create a program or source package</h3><p>If the <strong>Create a program</strong><span> area is available, describe the program you want and request a build. Wait for the result. If a downloadable ZIP</span><span> is offered, download it and inspect its contents. If no ZIP is produced, read the status message and check the</span><span> server's available capabilities. Do not assume every deployment has every build or execution feature.</span></p>
<h3>6.9 Approvals, budgets and safety</h3><p><span>Architect intentionally separates suggestions from approvals. Read what an approval is for before acting. A budget approval may ask</span><span> you to enter a new numeric limit; enter only a limit you understand and are willing to authorise. Reject or</span><span> cancel if you are unsure. Do not approve a task or budget just because the button is available.</span></p>
<h3>6.10 Google Drive and sign-out</h3><ul><li>Use the Google Drive control to connect if it is configured for this server. A Google sign-in window may open.</li><li><span>Project files are saved to Drive only when you use an explicit save action. The application does not silently publish</span><span> a project to GitHub or deploy it for you.</span></li><li>The connection may be available only while this browser tab is open. Read the status message in the workspace.</li><li>Choose <strong>More → Sign out</strong> when finished on a shared computer.</li></ul>
<h3>6.11 Delete a project</h3><p>In the project list, open the <strong>⋯</strong> menu next to a project and choose <strong>Delete project</strong><span>. Read the confirmation carefully. Deleting a project and its data cannot be undone. If you may need it later,</span><span> export or save the relevant files first.</span></p>

<h2 id="files">7. Files and attachments</h2>
<h3>7.1 Attach files to a conversation</h3><ol><li>Use <strong>Attach files</strong> near the message box, or open the available file/workspace control.</li><li><span>Select the files you want the assistant to consider. On supported desktop browsers, you may also be able to import</span><span> a whole project folder through </span><strong>More → Import → Project folder (desktop)</strong>.</li><li>For a project archive, attach one ZIP at a time. BiForge may unpack it into a local workspace.</li><li>Check the file list and remove anything irrelevant or sensitive.</li><li>In the workspace privacy panel, read the notice. Until you choose <strong>I understand — let the assistant read these files</strong>, the workspace is not included in the model context.</li><li><span>After confirming, tell the assistant exactly what to do with the files, such as “Summarise the README” or “Find the</span><span> configuration that controls the login page.”</span></li></ol>
<p><span>Readable text from selected files may be sent to the AI provider when you use the assistant. Other files may</span><span> be retained locally and described by name/type rather than read as text. Never include passwords, API keys, private keys, payment-card</span><span> details, identity documents, or other information you would not want processed by the model.</span></p>
<h3>7.2 Export a workspace</h3><ul><li><strong>Download all (.zip)</strong> downloads the files in the current workspace.</li><li><strong>Changes only</strong> downloads files that differ from the previous workspace version and a change list. It becomes available after a later version exists.</li><li><strong>Text diff</strong> downloads a readable text comparison with the previous version, when available.</li><li>Store downloads in a known folder and use clear filenames so you can find them later.</li></ul>
<h3>7.3 Google Drive</h3><p>Drive features are optional and depend on server configuration and your Google access. Use the Drive button or an explicit <strong>Save to Google Drive</strong><span> action. Read the status message to see whether a save succeeded. A cloud icon is not a guarantee that every</span><span> local file has been uploaded.</span></p>

<h2 id="topbar">8. Top bar, history, import and export</h2>
<h3>Mode</h3><p>Shows the current mode and opens the mode menu. Choose Conversational, Expert, or Architect if available. <strong>Back to mode selection</strong><span> returns to the mode chooser. When leaving Architect, your projects remain on the server, but confirm before leaving if</span><span> you have unsaved browser input.</span></p>
<h3>New</h3><p>Starts a new conversation/decision dialogue. It does not mean “delete everything.” Use History or export to return to older work.</p>
<h3>History</h3><p><span>Opens dialogues saved on this device. Select an item to reopen it. If History is disabled, the installation or current</span><span> browser state does not provide it. History on this device is not the same as a server-wide account history; do</span><span> not assume it appears on another device.</span></p>
<h3>More → Export all</h3><ul><li><strong>Backup (.json)</strong> — a machine-readable backup. Use this format when you want to import BiForge data again.</li><li><strong>Readable text (.md)</strong> — a text/Markdown copy that is easy to read or edit.</li><li><strong>Word</strong> — a document for editing or sharing in a word processor.</li><li><strong>PDF</strong> — a fixed-layout document for reading or sharing.</li><li><strong>Google Docs</strong> — sends an export to Google Docs when that integration is configured.</li></ul><p><span>Choose a format, then follow the browser's download or save prompt. Keep backup files somewhere safe. An export is a</span><span> copy; it does not automatically synchronise every future edit.</span></p>
<h3>More → Import</h3><ul><li><strong>From backup (.json)</strong> — select a BiForge backup JSON file. Review any import/merge information before relying on the result.</li><li><strong>Project folder (desktop)</strong> — available only in supported desktop browsers. It loads a folder into the local workspace; it does not deploy the project.</li><li><strong>From Google Drive</strong> — available when the Drive integration is provided. Select or connect the appropriate Drive source and check the status message.</li><li><strong>From a file as a new dialogue</strong><span> — imports a supported text, Word, or PDF document as a new dialogue context. The assistant can still misunderstand the</span><span> document, so verify important details.</span></li></ul>
<h3>Saved texts on this device</h3><p>Opens texts saved from chats on this device. This is useful for retrieving a piece of generated text without searching through a whole conversation.</p>
<h3>Your Gemini API key</h3><p><span>This optional setting may let you use your own Gemini API key if the installation supports it. Only use it</span><span> if you understand the provider account and billing implications. Never paste API keys into ordinary chat messages. If you do</span><span> not need this feature, leave it alone.</span></p>
<h3>New tab and Close in this guide</h3><p><strong>New tab</strong> opens a separate copy of this guide so you can keep it beside BiForge. <strong>Close</strong> returns you to your work. You can reopen this guide any time through <strong>More → User guide</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="privacy">9. Privacy and safe use</h2><ul><li><strong>Do not enter secrets.</strong><span> Avoid passwords, API keys, private keys, payment-card numbers, identity documents, and confidential information unless you have checked the applicable data-handling</span><span> rules and explicitly intend to share it.</span></li><li><strong>Review file access.</strong><span> The workspace asks for permission before its readable contents are made available to the assistant. Only grant it when you</span><span> are comfortable sharing those contents with the AI provider.</span></li><li><strong>Verify important claims.</strong> Check legal, medical, financial, security, employment, and other high-impact information against reliable sources or qualified professionals.</li><li><strong>Check generated code.</strong><span> A source ZIP is not proof that software is secure, correct, or ready for production. Test it, review dependencies and</span><span> configuration, and protect real user data.</span></li><li><strong>Review before approving.</strong> In the Expert and Architect agents, a suggestion, plan, task result, or generated file is not automatically correct or approved.</li><li><strong>Publishing is manual.</strong> BiForge does not automatically push your code to GitHub or deploy it to a hosting service. Follow the instructions and perform those actions yourself.</li><li><strong>Understand where things are stored.</strong><span> Conversational/Expert conversations and workspace files may be stored locally in your browser; Project data is stored on the server. Drive</span><span> actions depend on configuration. Export important work if you need a separate copy.</span></li><li><strong>Shared devices.</strong> Sign out of Architect and avoid leaving sensitive files or conversations open where others can access them.</li></ul>

<h2 id="troubleshooting">10. Troubleshooting</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What to try</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Architect is unavailable</strong></td><td>The server may have Architect disabled, or your account may not be authorised. Ask the BiForge administrator. Conversational and Expert may still work.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Sign-in fails</strong></td><td><span>Check that you are using the application password supplied by the administrator. Do not send it in chat. If it</span><span> still fails, contact the administrator.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Run analysis is disabled</strong></td><td>Confirm the project description first, then enter a non-empty analysis task.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Confirm/Reject buttons are missing</strong></td><td>They appear only for items that are still proposed. Already reviewed items may not offer the same actions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A task cannot start</strong></td><td><span>Check its status and any displayed blocked reason. The plan may need approval, a required capability may be unavailable, or</span><span> a limit may have been reached.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Task completed but I am unsure it worked</strong></td><td>Inspect the actual output and acceptance criteria. Do not mark it passed until you can enter concrete evidence.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>No source ZIP appears</strong></td><td><span>Read the build status and note. Code generation may be disabled or unavailable in this deployment, or the request may</span><span> not have produced a package.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Drive is not connected</strong></td><td><span>Check whether Drive is configured for this server, allow the sign-in window if expected, and try the explicit Connect/Save action</span><span> again. Read the resulting status message.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Import or export does not work</strong></td><td>Check the file type, browser download permissions, available storage, and any error message. Use JSON for a BiForge backup that you plan to re-import.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Save rejected or data changed</strong></td><td>The project may have changed in another tab or window. Reload the project and review the latest values before trying again.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The assistant misunderstood me</strong></td><td><span>Explain what it misunderstood, provide the missing fact, and ask it to revise the answer. For a project, also update</span><span> the project description if the underlying requirement has changed.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The page seems stuck</strong></td><td><span>Wait briefly, check your connection, and read any error/status message. If you reload, be aware that unsaved text in the</span><span> current form may be lost.</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h2 id="glossary">11. Small glossary</h2><dl><dt>AI assistant / model</dt><dd>The software that reads your request and generates a response. It can be useful but can also be wrong.</dd><dt>Prompt</dt><dd>The instruction or question you send to the assistant.</dd><dt>Context</dt><dd>The information available to the assistant for the current task, such as your message and files you have allowed it to read.</dd><dt>Assumption</dt><dd>Something treated as true for the purpose of an answer even though it has not been confirmed.</dd><dt>Requirement</dt><dd>Something the project must do or a condition it must satisfy.</dd><dt>Artifact</dt><dd>A saved file or output produced during project work.</dd><dt>Plan</dt><dd>A proposed sequence of tasks. In Architect it requires human approval before tasks can run.</dd><dt>Task validation</dt><dd>A human check of whether the task output meets its acceptance criteria.</dd><dt>Source ZIP</dt><dd>A downloadable archive of source files. It is not a live or deployed application.</dd><dt>Deploy / publish</dt><dd>Make a software application available on a hosting service or to real users. This is a separate step from generating a ZIP.</dd></dl>


<h2 id="lab-escape">12. Your first project: build Lab Escape</h2>
<p>You are going to try a real project from beginning to end: a small browser escape-room game called <strong>Lab Escape: The Last Keycard</strong><span>. The goal is to learn how to describe an idea, review what BiForge understood, request a build, test the</span><span> result, and improve it. You do not need to know how to code.</span></p>
<div class="callout"><strong>A realistic promise:</strong><span> this is a deliberately small project, not a guarantee that every BiForge installation can generate a working game in one</span><span> click. Architect features depend on the server configuration and generated code can contain mistakes. If a step or button</span><span> is unavailable, follow the capability notes in Section 6 and ask the administrator which project-building features are enabled.</span></div>
<h3>12.1 What you are going to build</h3>
<p><span>You are trapped in a small science laboratory. To escape, you must discover a clue, enter a code into an</span><span> electrical panel, restore power, obtain an access card from a terminal, and unlock the exit.</span></p>
<p>The first version should contain:</p><ul><li>One screen showing a single laboratory room.</li><li>Four clickable objects: a desk, an electrical panel, a terminal, and an exit door.</li><li>A simple puzzle whose clue is found at the desk.</li><li>A small inventory or status area showing what you have discovered.</li><li>Clear messages explaining what happens after each action.</li><li>A victory screen when the exit is unlocked.</li><li>A <strong>Restart</strong> button so you can play again.</li></ul>
<p>To keep the first attempt manageable, do <strong>not</strong> add accounts, online scores, a database, multiplayer, paid services, external APIs, or complicated animation. You can add extras after the basic game works.</p>
<h3>12.2 Open Architect</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Architect</strong> from the <strong>Mode</strong> menu.</li><li>If BiForge asks you to sign in, use the application password supplied by the administrator. Never paste that password into the AI chat.</li><li>Use the guided start to describe the idea in ordinary language. There is no separate mode choice.</li><li><span>If Architect is not available, do not try to bypass the restriction. Use the available mode</span><span> or ask the administrator whether project creation and ZIP generation are enabled.</span></li></ol>
<h3>12.3 Describe the game in your own words</h3><p>In the <strong>What do you want to build?</strong> field, enter the following request. You can copy and paste it as written:</p>
<div class="example"><p><span>Build a small browser game called “Lab Escape: The Last Keycard”. The player is locked in a science laboratory and</span><span> must escape by solving one simple puzzle. Make a single-screen escape-room game with four clickable objects: a desk, an electrical</span><span> panel, a terminal, and an exit door. The desk reveals a clue. The player uses the clue to enter a</span><span> code into the electrical panel and restore power. The powered terminal provides an access card. The player can use the</span><span> access card to unlock the exit and win. Show clear messages after every action, show collected items or progress, include</span><span> a Restart button, and display a victory message when the puzzle is complete. Use a simple, attractive science-lab theme that</span><span> works on desktop and mobile screens. Keep the game small and easy to understand. Do not add accounts, a database,</span><span> online features, paid services, external APIs, or unnecessary dependencies. The game should run locally if the generated package supports that.</span><span> Include clear instructions for running and testing it.</span></p></div>
<p>This is a <strong>prompt</strong><span>: a written instruction for the AI. It describes the goal, the main interactions, and what to leave out. You</span><span> do not need to know the technical details to ask for a useful result.</span></p>
<h3>12.4 Choose the product type and answer the setup questions</h3><ol><li>Choose the closest product type offered by BiForge, such as a small web application or browser-based tool. The exact choices may vary by installation.</li><li>If asked whether users need to sign in, choose <strong>No</strong> for this game.</li><li>If asked whether the project needs a database, choose <strong>No</strong>.</li><li>Choose <strong>English</strong> for the interface, unless you want to practise another language.</li><li>If asked about technical setup, choose <strong>Choose for me</strong> if you are unsure.</li><li>Add or keep these constraints if the form offers them: <strong>small first version</strong>, <strong>mobile-friendly</strong>, <strong>no external APIs</strong>, <strong>no paid services</strong>, and <strong>no user accounts</strong>.</li><li>Continue to the project summary.</li></ol><p><span>Do not worry if a label is slightly different. Read the available options and choose the closest match. If a</span><span> question is unclear, ask BiForge to explain it before proceeding.</span></p>
<h3>12.5 Review the project summary before creating anything</h3><p>Read the summary slowly. Check these points:</p><ul><li>Is the project a small browser game, not a large online game?</li><li>Does it include the desk clue, code panel, powered terminal, access card, and exit?</li><li>Does the game have a clear win condition and a Restart button?</li><li>Does it avoid accounts, a database, external APIs, and paid services?</li><li>Does it include instructions for running and testing the result?</li></ul><p>If something is wrong, go back and correct the description. You can also ask BiForge:</p><div class="example"><p><span>Please revise the project summary so it matches my original request. Keep the first version small. Do not add features</span><span> I did not ask for. List any requirement you cannot support or are uncertain about.</span></p></div><p>When the summary matches your idea, choose <strong>Create project &amp; build ZIP</strong><span> if that action is available. Wait for the operation to finish and read the status message. Do not assume the</span><span> build succeeded just because you clicked the button.</span></p>
<h3>12.6 If you use the Professional path instead</h3><p>If you chose <strong>Professional</strong>, create a project with a recognisable name such as <strong>Lab Escape — The Last Keycard</strong> and set its goal to <strong>Create a small, locally runnable browser escape-room game for beginners</strong>. Save and confirm the project description before running analysis.</p><p>For the analysis task, you can use:</p><div class="example"><p><span>Review the requirements for Lab Escape: The Last Keycard. Identify missing requirements, likely implementation risks, and a small first-version plan.</span><span> Keep the game to one room, four clickable objects, one code puzzle, an inventory/status area, a victory state, and a</span><span> Restart button. Do not add accounts, a database, online features, external APIs, or paid services. Separate confirmed requirements from assumptions</span><span> and tell me if any requested capability may not be available.</span></p></div><p><span>Read the results and confirm only suggestions that are accurate. Reject suggestions that change the game into something larger or</span><span> different. Then create a simple plan if that option is available. Review every task and approval before starting it. If</span><span> a task asks you to approve an action you do not understand, stop and ask for an explanation.</span></p>
<h3>12.7 Download and inspect the result</h3><p>If BiForge provides <strong>Download source ZIP</strong>, download it to a folder you can find again, such as <strong>Downloads/Lab-Escape</strong>. A ZIP is a package of source files; it is not automatically a published website.</p><ol><li>Open the downloaded ZIP in your computer's file manager.</li><li>Extract it to a new folder. Do not edit files while they are still inside the ZIP.</li><li>Look for a <strong>README</strong> or other run instructions. Read them before opening or running anything.</li><li>If the package is a simple static web game and includes an <code>index.html</code>, the instructions may tell you to open that file in a browser. If it includes <code>package.json</code>, a build folder, or other setup files, follow the package's instructions instead; it may require a local development server or installed tools.</li><li><span>If you are unsure, return to BiForge and ask it to explain the exact files it generated and the safest</span><span> way to run the game on your computer. Do not run unknown scripts with administrator privileges.</span></li></ol><p>If no ZIP is offered, read the displayed status and the <strong>Code package</strong> or <strong>Create a program</strong><span> notes in Section 6. This may mean code generation is not enabled for this BiForge installation. Do not claim the</span><span> game has been built until you can inspect an actual output.</span></p>
<h3>12.8 Play-test the game like a detective</h3><p>Try the game from a fresh start. Use this checklist and record what happens:</p><ol><li><strong>The desk:</strong> clicking it reveals a clue or code hint.</li><li><strong>Wrong code:</strong> entering an incorrect code does not restore power.</li><li><strong>Correct code:</strong> entering the correct code restores power and changes the game's status.</li><li><strong>The terminal:</strong> before power is restored, it should explain why it cannot provide the access card. After power is restored, it should provide the card.</li><li><strong>The exit:</strong> the door must stay locked until the player has the access card.</li><li><strong>Victory:</strong> using the card at the exit completes the game and shows a clear victory message.</li><li><strong>Restart:</strong> the Restart button returns the game to its initial state; the clue, power, card, and victory state are reset.</li><li><strong>Usability:</strong> text is readable, controls are clear, and the game is usable on a narrow browser window as well as a desktop screen.</li></ol><p><span>If any item fails, the game is not finished yet. This is normal: generating a first version is the start</span><span> of testing, not the end.</span></p>
<h3>12.9 Ask BiForge to fix one problem at a time</h3><p>Describe the observed problem, not just “the game is broken”. For example, if the door opens too early, send this request:</p><div class="example"><p><span>Fix this specific bug: the exit door currently opens before the player has collected the access card. The door must</span><span> remain locked until the access-card item is in the inventory. After the change, explain which file or logic was changed</span><span> and how I can test that the door stays locked before collecting the card and opens afterward. Do not change</span><span> unrelated game features.</span></p></div><p><span>If the game is generated as a project that supports follow-up code changes, ask BiForge to make the fix and</span><span> provide a new package or explain where the changed files are. Replace your local copy only after you have saved</span><span> a backup of the version that worked. Then repeat the relevant checklist items.</span></p>
<h3>12.10 Make your first improvement</h3><p>Once the core game works, choose <strong>one</strong> improvement. Do not ask for everything at once. Here are good beginner-sized ideas:</p><ul><li>Add a timer that starts when the game begins.</li><li>Add two difficulty settings with different puzzle codes.</li><li>Add a second clue hidden in the room.</li><li>Improve the colours, typography, and laboratory background.</li><li>Add simple sound effects with a mute button.</li><li>Add a short hint button for players who get stuck.</li></ul><p>Use a request like this:</p><div class="example"><p><span>Please add a Hint button to Lab Escape. It should give one small clue at a time without revealing the</span><span> full answer immediately. Keep all existing game rules working. After making the change, tell me how to test the hint</span><span> button and confirm that the player can still win and restart the game. Do not add external services or unrelated</span><span> features.</span></p></div><p><span>After each improvement, replay the relevant tests. If the change breaks something that previously worked, ask BiForge to fix the</span><span> regression before adding another feature.</span></p>
<h3>12.11 What you have learned</h3><p>You have practised a complete small-project workflow:</p><ol><li>Describe a goal in plain language.</li><li>State the important requirements and limits.</li><li>Review the project description before building.</li><li>Inspect the generated files and follow their run instructions.</li><li>Test the actual behaviour instead of trusting a success message.</li><li>Report a specific bug, make a small change, and test again.</li></ol><p><span>You do not need to understand every line of code to begin. You do need to understand what the game</span><span> should do, inspect the result, and keep control of decisions. That is the central habit this example is meant to</span><span> teach.</span></p>

<div class="callout final"><strong>When in doubt, take the safe next step:</strong><span> ask BiForge to explain, review the result, and do not approve or publish anything you do not understand. You do</span><span> not need to be an expert to use BiForge well; clear questions and careful review are enough to get started.</span></div>
`;

const GUIDE_CSS = String.raw`
:root{color-scheme:dark}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif;line-height:1.68;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:24px 22px 56px;background:#0d1b2a;color:#e6edf7;font-size:15px}h1{font-size:2rem;line-height:1.2;margin:.2em 0 .55em}h2{font-size:1.4rem;line-height:1.3;margin:2.2em 0 .65em;padding-bottom:.4em;border-bottom:1px solid #28527c;color:#f1f6ff;scroll-margin-top:16px}h3{font-size:1.08rem;margin:1.5em 0 .4em;color:#9fd8ff}p{margin:.6em 0 .9em}li{margin:.35em 0}ul,ol{padding-left:1.5em}a{color:#8fc8ff;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px}strong{color:#fff}code{background:#07111f;padding:2px 5px;border-radius:4px}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin:1em 0 1.4em;font-size:.93rem}th,td{border:1px solid #31506e;padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}th{background:#142b43;color:#fff}tr:nth-child(even) td{background:#0b1929}.lead{font-size:1.12rem;color:#d1e2f5}.callout{margin:1.2em 0;padding:14px 16px;border:1px solid #3d7fc4;border-left:4px solid #78baff;border-radius:8px;background:#0b2545}.callout.final{margin-top:2em}.example{border:1px solid #294663;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 14px;background:#0a1726}.toc{border:1px solid #294663;border-radius:10px;background:#0a1726;padding:16px 20px;margin:1.5em 0}.toc ol{columns:2;column-gap:30px}.toc li{break-inside:avoid}.toc a{text-decoration:none}.toc a:hover{text-decoration:underline}dt{font-weight:700;color:#9fd8ff;margin-top:.8em}dd{margin-left:1.2em;color:#d3deeb} @media(max-width:620px){body{padding:18px 15px 40px;font-size:14px}h1{font-size:1.65rem}h2{font-size:1.25rem}.toc ol{columns:1}table{font-size:.85rem}th,td{padding:7px}.guide-shell{padding:12px!important}}
`;

export function UserGuideModal({ open, onClose }: { open: boolean; onClose: () => void }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!open) return;
    const onKey = (e: KeyboardEvent) => { if (e.key === 'Escape') onClose(); };
    document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
    const prevOverflow = document.body.style.overflow;
    const prevPos = document.body.style.position;
    document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
    document.body.style.position = 'relative';
    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
      document.body.style.overflow = prevOverflow;
      document.body.style.position = prevPos;
    };
  }, [open, onClose]);

  const openInNewTab = useCallback(() => {
    const html = `<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><title>BiForge User Guide</title><style>${GUIDE_CSS}</style></head><body>${GUIDE_HTML}</body></html>`;
    const blob = new Blob([html], { type: 'text/html;charset=utf-8' });
    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    window.open(url, '_blank', 'noopener,noreferrer');
    window.setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(url), 60_000);
  }, []);

  if (!open) return null;
  return (
    <div className="user-guide-root" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="BiForge User Guide" style={{ position: 'fixed', inset: 0, zIndex: 3000, display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', background: '#07111f', color: '#e6edf7', width: '100%', height: '100%', maxHeight: '100dvh' }}>
      <style>{`.user-guide-content{font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif;line-height:1.68;font-size:15px;color:#e6edf7}.user-guide-content h1{font-size:27px;line-height:1.2;margin:.2em 0 .55em}.user-guide-content h2{font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;margin:2em 0 .65em;padding-bottom:.4em;border-bottom:1px solid #28527c;color:#f1f6ff;scroll-margin-top:10px}.user-guide-content h3{font-size:16px;margin:1.5em 0 .4em;color:#9fd8ff}.user-guide-content p{margin:.6em 0 .9em}.user-guide-content li{margin:.35em 0}.user-guide-content ul,.user-guide-content ol{padding-left:1.5em}.user-guide-content a{color:#8fc8ff;text-underline-offset:2px}.user-guide-content strong{color:#fff}.user-guide-content table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin:1em 0 1.4em;font-size:.92em}.user-guide-content th,.user-guide-content td{border:1px solid #31506e;padding:9px 10px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}.user-guide-content th{background:#142b43}.user-guide-content tr:nth-child(even) td{background:#0b1929}.user-guide-content .lead{font-size:1.08em;color:#d1e2f5}.user-guide-content .callout{margin:1.2em 0;padding:14px 16px;border:1px solid #3d7fc4;border-left:4px solid #78baff;border-radius:8px;background:#0b2545}.user-guide-content .callout.final{margin-top:2em}.user-guide-content .example,.user-guide-content .toc{border:1px solid #294663;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;background:#0a1726}.user-guide-content .toc{padding:16px 20px;margin:1.5em 0}.user-guide-content .toc ol{columns:2;column-gap:26px}.user-guide-content .toc li{break-inside:avoid}.user-guide-content .toc a{text-decoration:none}.user-guide-content dt{font-weight:700;color:#9fd8ff;margin-top:.8em}.user-guide-content dd{margin-left:1.2em;color:#d3deeb}@media(max-width:620px){.user-guide-content{font-size:14px}.user-guide-content .toc ol{columns:1}.user-guide-content table{font-size:.84em}.user-guide-content th,.user-guide-content td{padding:6px}}`}</style>
      <div className="user-guide-toolbar" style={{ flexShrink: 0, display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', justifyContent: 'space-between', gap: 10, padding: '12px 14px', paddingTop: 'max(12px, env(safe-area-inset-top))', borderBottom: '1px solid #1f4f86', background: '#0d1b2a' }}>
        <h1 style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: 17, display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: 8, fontWeight: 600 }}><CircleHelp size={18} aria-hidden /> BiForge User Guide</h1>
        <div style={{ display: 'flex', gap: 8, flexShrink: 0 }}>
          <button type="button" className="ghost" onClick={openInNewTab} title="Open this guide in a new browser tab"><ExternalLink size={14} /> <span className="lbl">New tab</span></button>
          <button type="button" className="ghost" onClick={onClose} aria-label="Close user guide"><X size={16} /> <span className="lbl">Close</span></button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div className="user-guide-scroll" style={{ flex: '1 1 auto', minHeight: 0, overflowY: 'auto', overflowX: 'hidden', WebkitOverflowScrolling: 'touch', overscrollBehavior: 'contain', padding: '16px 16px max(32px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom))', maxWidth: 940, width: '100%', margin: '0 auto', boxSizing: 'border-box' }}>
        <article className="user-guide-content" dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: GUIDE_HTML }} />
        <p style={{ color: '#8fa6c2', fontSize: 12, marginTop: 28, paddingBottom: 20 }}>BiForge User Guide · You can reopen this manual at any time from More → User guide.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

export default UserGuideModal;
